      FR-1225: both sides of a card in one capture. "Another photo" and not "the back", because a
      fold-out card has more than two sides and the control is the same one either way.
    -->
    <string name="card_add_photo">Add another photo</string>
    <string name="card_reading_photo">Reading…</string>
    <string name="card_reading_photo_note">Reading the photo you just took. Save is off until it is in.</string>
    <!--
      FR-207's pattern, one pillar over: a photograph that yielded nothing is invisible otherwise.
      Shown only when a photograph gave no text — the cap is enforced by withdrawing the control,
      so nothing is ever taken and then ignored.
    -->
    <!--
      FR-1226: the only control in this application that sends an image anywhere. The label says
      what happens rather than naming a setting, and "this photo" rather than "the card" because
      with FR-1225 there may be several and it is the first that is used.
    -->
    <string name="card_attach_photo">Use this photo as the contact’s picture</string>
    <string name="card_photo_failed">The contact was saved, but Google would not take the picture. Everything else is there.</string>
    <string name="card_photo_not_held">No connection, so the picture cannot be sent. The contact will be saved without it — Latch never keeps the photo on your phone.</string>
    <string name="card_photos_read">%1$d of %2$d photos read. One gave Latch nothing — retake it if it matters.</string>
    <!--
      FR-1201a: the photograph was taken from Latch's own card button, so the user has already
      said this is a card. Nothing came back — no code, no text at all — which is a different
      thing from a card that read badly, and is nearly always the photograph rather than the app.
    -->
    <string name="card_photo_unreadable">Nothing could be read from that photo. Try again with the card filling more of the frame, in better light.</string>
    <!-- FR-1202: the action on the capture sheet. Two labels, because a detected card and an
         image that merely might be one are different offers. -->
    <string name="capture_card_detected">This looks like a contact card — save as contact</string>
    <string name="capture_card_available">Save as contact instead</string>

    <string name="capture_dismiss">Close</string>
    <string name="capture_save">Save</string>
    <string name="capture_saving">Saving…</string>
    <string name="capture_saved">Saved to %1$s</string>
    <!-- FR-803: the same message was captured before, so nothing was written this time. -->
    <string name="capture_already_saved">Already saved. Nothing was written again.</string>

    <!--
        FR-804. Both dates are shown because the user is being asked to move one thing to
        another, and cannot answer without seeing both. The weekday in each is computed from
        the date rather than taken from the captured text, which may contradict it.
    -->
    <string name="capture_reschedule_offer">This looks like a reschedule. \"%1$s\" is already saved for %2$s. Move it to %3$s?</string>
    <string name="capture_reschedule_update">Update</string>
    <string name="capture_reschedule_create_new">New</string>
    <string name="capture_reschedule_no_date">no date</string>
    <!-- The duplicate check gave up before reading every task; see the reading against FR-803. -->
    <string name="capture_saved_unchecked">Saved. Latch could not check every task for a duplicate, so this may be a second copy.</string>
    <!-- FR-807: the undo offer, carrying the seconds it has left so it visibly runs out. -->
    <string name="capture_undo">Undo (%1$d)</string>
    <string name="capture_undoing">Removing…</string>
    <string name="capture_undone">Removed. Nothing was left in your Google account.</string>
    <!-- NFR-303: an undo that failed says the item is still there, and where to go for it. -->
    <string name="capture_undo_failed">Could not remove this from Google. It is still there — remove it in Google Calendar or Google Tasks.</string>
    <string name="capture_undo_failed_partial">Removed %1$d of %2$d items. The rest are still in your Google account — remove them in Google Calendar or Google Tasks.</string>
    <!-- FR-806: no network, so the capture is on disk and goes to Google when there is one.
         Deliberately does not say "saved" — nothing is in the account yet. -->
    <string name="capture_queued">No connection. Saved on this phone and will go to %1$s when you are back online.</string>
    <!-- NFR-303: a failure says what happened and what was not saved. -->
    <string name="capture_save_error">Could not save to Google. Check your connection and try again — nothing was saved.</string>
    <string name="capture_save_no_destination">Finish setting up Latch before saving.</string>
    <string name="capture_save_needs_date">Latch found a time but no day, and cannot save without one yet.</string>
    <!-- FR-805: the link back to where a capture came from, written into the item itself. -->
    <string name="capture_source_link">Captured from %1$s</string>
    <!-- FR-512, interim: confidence is shown rather than blocking the save. -->
    <string name="capture_low_confidence">Latch is not confident about this one — check it before saving.</string>
    <!--
      FR-512, FR-506 rows 3 and 4, AC-03: the capture is going to the Capture Inbox rather than
      to Google. The button says so, because a Save button that quietly put something somewhere
      else would be the routing decision hidden from the person making it.
    -->
    <string name="capture_add_to_inbox">Add to Inbox</string>
    <string name="capture_sent_to_inbox">Added to your Inbox on this phone. Nothing was written to Google yet.</string>
    <string name="capture_route_undated">No date found, so this goes to your Inbox. Latch never guesses one — give it a date there.</string>
    <string name="capture_route_low_confidence">Latch is not confident about this one, so it goes to your Inbox to check first.</string>
    <string name="capture_route_incomplete">There is a time here but no day. This goes to your Inbox, where you can give it one.</string>
    <!-- FR-804 offline: the reschedule question needs a network to answer and a user to ask. -->
    <string name="capture_route_reschedule_offline">This looks like a change to something already saved, and Latch cannot check while offline. It goes to your Inbox so nothing is written twice.</string>
    <string name="capture_nothing_shared">Nothing was shared with Latch</string>
    <string name="capture_clipboard_empty">The clipboard is empty. Copy some text, then tap the tile.</string>

    <!--
      FR-215 and FR-207: on-device OCR. NFR-102 is why capture_extracting exists at all — the
      screen renders and fills in rather than waiting, so there has to be something to say
      while it waits.
    -->
    <string name="capture_extracting">Reading the text…</string>
    <!--
      FR-207 and NFR-102: a document is a wait with a number on it. The second number is how
      many pages will be read, not how many the document has — this is a progress line, and
      counting to fourteen while stopping at ten would show a bar that never fills. The cap
      itself is reported afterwards by capture_pdf_capped, which is where FR-207 puts it.
    -->
    <string name="capture_extracting_page">Reading page %1$d of %2$d…</string>
    <!-- FR-1201a: the same wait, named for what the user actually asked for. -->
    <string name="capture_extracting_card">Reading the card…</string>
    <string name="capture_ocr_unreadable">Latch could not open that file. Try sharing it again, or share the text instead.</string>
    <string name="capture_ocr_no_text">No text found in that image.</string>
    <string name="capture_ocr_failed">Latch could not read that image. Nothing was sent anywhere — the reading happens on your phone.</string>
    <!--
      FR-207's cap, reported rather than applied silently. The requirement is explicit that a
      search which stopped looking must say so, because the user cannot otherwise tell it
      apart from there having been nothing on page eleven.
    -->
    <string name="capture_pdf_capped">First %1$d of %2$d pages read.</string>


    <!-- FR-806: the queue, visible to the user. -->
    <plurals name="home_queue_waiting">
        <item quantity="one">%1$d capture waiting to be saved to Google</item>
        <item quantity="other">%1$d captures waiting to be saved to Google</item>
    </plurals>
    <!--
      FR-806a: the queue is held because Google wants a sign-in, not because the network is
      down. It is the one stuck-queue state the user can actually clear, so it says what to do
      rather than what happened, and it sits beside the count rather than replacing it — the
      captures are still safe and still waiting, which is the reassuring half.
    -->
    <string name="home_queue_needs_sign_in">Sign in to Google again to save these. Tap Sign in below; nothing has been lost.</string>
    <string name="home_grant_needs_sign_in">Latch needs you to sign in to Google again. Until you do, new captures will be held on this phone instead of saved.</string>
    <string name="home_sign_in">Sign in</string>
    <!-- NFR-303: an entry that will not come right on its own says so rather than sitting
         in the count looking like it is still on its way. -->
    <plurals name="home_queue_given_up">
        <item quantity="one">%1$d could not be saved.</item>
        <item quantity="other">%1$d could not be saved.</item>
    </plurals>
    <!--
      FR-806: the manual retry the requirement's own note recorded as missing until Settings
      exists. It also revives an entry that had been given up on — the user has usually done
      something between the failure and the tap, and refusing to try is worse than trying.
    -->
    <string name="home_queue_retry">Retry now</string>

    <!--
      FR-704: an unobtrusive count of Inbox items, and nothing at all when there are none. The
